<h1>Backup &amp; restore</h1>
<p class="lead">Your whole site is one SQL file (<code>.pod</code>, a plain SQLite database, only renamed). Back it up or move it to another server from the admin in two clicks.</p>

<h2>Download a backup</h2>
<p>Settings → <strong>Pod</strong> → <strong>Backup &amp; restore</strong> → <em>Download SQL file</em> (admins only). You get a consistent copy of the live database as <code>content-&lt;date&gt;.pod</code>, without active sessions. Media stored in the pod are inside the file; media on S3 are not.</p>

<h2>Automatic backups</h2>
<p>The admin copies the pod into a <code>backups/</code> folder next to it, by default <strong>every 7 days</strong> (checked at startup and every six hours), and keeps the newest four. <em>Back up now</em> in the same card does it on demand. Tune it with environment variables:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>ORBITER_BACKUP_DAYS</code></td><td>7</td><td>Days between automatic backups, <code>0</code> turns them off</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ORBITER_BACKUP_KEEP</code></td><td>4</td><td>How many automatic (and manual) copies to keep</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ORBITER_BACKUP_DIR</code></td><td><code>&lt;pod folder&gt;/backups</code></td><td>Where the copies go</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ORBITER_POD_WARN_MB</code></td><td>60</td><td>Size above which the dashboard and the card warn</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>With automatic backups off, the dashboard reminds you when the last backup is older than two weeks. A copy on the same disk protects against mistakes, not against losing the server: download one now and then.</p>

<h2>Keeping the pod small</h2>
<p>The card shows the file size and how much of it is media stored inside the pod. If you want to stay below a limit, choose a media backend under <strong>Settings → Media</strong> (local folder, S3 or GitHub) and press <em>Move media out of the pod…</em>: the files are uploaded one by one (a file that fails stays where it is), then the pod is shrunk with <code>VACUUM</code>. Make a backup first. Moving to S3 or GitHub needs working credentials; the local folder is the simplest.</p>

<h2>Restore or move</h2>
<p><em>Restore…</em> takes a <code>.pod</code> file. The server checks it first (SQLite, integrity check, required tables, at least one admin) and refuses anything else. It then keeps the current pod next to it as <code>&lt;pod&gt;.before-restore-&lt;time&gt;</code> (the last three are kept), swaps the new file in and signs you out.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Credentials saved encrypted need the same <code>ORBITER_SECRET</code> on the new server.</li>
  <li>A pod from <a href="/docs/lander">Lander</a> works too; its admin passwords are PHP hashes, so reset the password with the CLI if you cannot sign in.</li>
  <li>The upload size is capped by <code>ORBITER_MAX_UPLOAD_MB</code> (default 100).</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout"><strong>Backups are sensitive.</strong> They contain password hashes and saved credentials. Store them privately and never commit a <code>.pod</code> to a public repository.</div>

<h2>API</h2>
<pre>GET  /api/backup           — download the pod (admin)
POST /api/backup/restore   — multipart field "file" (admin)
GET  /api/backup/status    — size, last backup, automatic backups
POST /api/backup/run       — back up now into the backup folder
POST /api/backup/offload-media?limit=25 — move media out of the pod
POST /api/backup/vacuum    — shrink the pod file</pre>

